"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of 'Mistral Large 4', a next-generation open-weight multimodal model with 1 trillion parameters specialized in coding, agent workflows, cybersecurity, and more. ML4 (Mistral Large 4) is a natively multimodal model utilizing 52 billion active parameters out of 1 trillion total parameters, scheduled to be released as open weights at the end of this month. It was trained from scratch using 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its own European datacenters, incorporating multilingual data across more than 160 languages. It delivers state-of-the-art open-model performance in enterprise environments such as cybersecurity, finance, and legal, enabling customized security research and policy enforcement through self-deployment. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral is opening a new hub in Munich, Germany, ramping up full-scale development of industrial and physics AI for manufacturing and heavy industry in Germany, Europe's largest industrial powerhouse. Mistral is opening a hub in Munich, Germany, deploying Physics AI and Industrial AI research teams along with field engineers to support enterprise partners. Mistral is actively pursuing concrete collaboration projects with major German conglomerates, such as crash simulation and engineering AI partnerships with BMW, and industrial AI implementation with Siemens Energy. To secure European AI sovereignty, Mistral emphasized building 1 GW of European computing capacity by 2030, alongside an open-weight architecture capable of running independently on customer infrastructure. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral AI and Mozilla have partnered to integrate Mistral models into Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Smart Window'. Mozilla's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window (beta)', is powered by Mistral models. It will first be available to users in France and North America, with support expanding to the UK and Germany later this year. It applies fine-tuning tailored to regional languages and cultural contexts, with privacy protection enabled by default, including unsaved conversations and zero data retention. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral AI and Cloudera collaborate to bring specialized sovereign AI intelligence for enterprise data to on-premises and cloud environments. Mistral AI models are integrated with the Cloudera hybrid data platform, supporting inference execution across on-premises, public cloud, and air-gapped environments. Enables enterprises to train customized AI models using proprietary data within a controlled environment while maintaining ownership of their data and intelligence. Focuses on addressing the demand for sovereign AI that allows customers to directly control data, compute, and model ownership across the board. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Presents a case study where Mistral utilized AI agents to successfully migrate a complex 40,000-line legacy Fortran 77 reservoir simulator into a modern C++ system for a European energy company. Going beyond mere syntax translation, the migration was performed by refactoring the architecture from a procedural language to object-oriented C++ and building a harness to verify numerical consistency. Addressed a legacy scientific computing codebase lacking documentation and test suites by having AI agents document it upfront and applying a phased workflow. Balanced AI agent autonomy with human oversight by engineers to ensure high-quality, maintainable code. Trump’s attempt to rename AI is looking awfully artificial An article covering President Donald Trump's administrative and linguistic rebranding attempt to replace the term 'Artificial Intelligence (AI)' with 'Super Intelligence,' as well as the alignment of tech industry leaders with this move. President Trump issued an executive order mandating the rebranding of the technology to 'super intelligence,' claiming that the word 'artificial' sounds fake. Government-wide terminology transition is proceeding swiftly, including the renaming of a government agency to CAISSI and the adoption of the term 'super intelligence' in Department of Justice press releases. Major tech leaders including Elon Musk, Jeff Bezos, Jensen Huang, and Sam Altman have aligned with or expressed favorable stances toward Trump's push to use the term 'super intelligence.' Instinct was the buzziest AI agent around — can it survive Muse? An analysis of startup Instinct, which maintains its competitive edge through differentiated everyday assistance capabilities and a text-based interface amidst the onslaught of AI agents from Big Tech companies. Despite an invite-only launch and a lack of marketing, startup Instinct garnered significant attention for its text-based interface and ability to handle everyday tasks. Even with the release of similar agents from tech giants, such as OpenAI's Dots and Meta's Muse, Instinct demonstrated comparable performance in internal tests and achieved a valuation of $10 billion as of late September. Founder Noah Shinn emphasized that, unlike large corporations developing general-purpose products, Instinct's core competitive advantage lies in its singular focus on a personal assistant deeply integrated into daily life. OpenAI doubles down on decision to fire three AI safety researchers OpenAI reiterated that its decision to dismiss three AI safety researchers was not due to raising safety concerns, but rather because of a significant breach of trust, including violations of policies regarding the handling of sensitive information. OpenAI stated that three safety researchers—Jasmine Wang, Tomek Korbak, and Mikita Balesni—were terminated for violating policies on handling sensitive information. In an open letter, the dismissed researchers claimed that they were fired for raising AI safety issues and demanded a transparent explanation. OpenAI stated that an internal investigation uncovered violations beyond what was mentioned in the letter, maintaining firmly that raising safety concerns was not the reason for their dismissal. Meet the Underdog Saluki 27B: A 2-bit Qwen3.8-27B That Beats the Original at Tool Calling The Underdog team at Conway Research unveiled Saluki 27B, a local agent model that quantized Qwen3.8-27B down to a 2-bit 7.89GB GGUF while boosting tool-calling performance beyond the original model. Qwen3.8-27B (54GB) was compressed into a 7.89GB 2-bit mixed-precision GGUF, enabling full offloading and operation on a single GPU in a pure llama.cpp environment. By focusing on preserving tool-calling capabilities during compression, it scored 42 points on a parallel tool-calling benchmark, outperforming the original model's 35 points. While retaining an average of 96% performance across nine major benchmarks compared to the original, its performance in competition-level complex mathematics and reasoning dropped by roughly 12 to 18 points. Google Cloud Launches Gemini Agent, One Universal Agent for Enterprise Work Google Cloud has launched 'Gemini Agent,' a universal enterprise agent designed to perform tasks across enterprise operations through a single prompt and API. It acts as a delegation layer covering Q&A, knowledge work, media generation, and code authoring and execution through a unified interface and API. It supports four memory tiers—session, semantic, procedural, and episodic memory—along with multi-agent orchestration and MCP server integration. It routes tasks to optimized models, utilizing not only Google's proprietary models (Argon, Flash, Omni, Gemma) but also Anthropic's Claude models. Google Research RRSI Guide: Mastering Self-Improving AI Agents A tutorial on implementing Google Research's RRSI framework, which empowers AI agents to rewrite and improve the harness around a fixed LLM without overfitting. RRSI (Regularized Recursive Self-Improvement) is an approach where the model weights remain fixed while the agent harness—including prompts, tools, memory, and control flow—is autonomously modified and evolved. To prevent overfitting, Python-based rules such as calibrated noise bands, annealed edit budgets, deterministic leakage checks, and edit history tracking are used to determine whether to accept modifications. Through a custom-built simulation environment, RRSI's decision process can be benchmarked against ground truth and evaluated against unregularized search performance. Build Your First MCP Server in Python (Stateless Spec Edition) An article introducing how to build an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server in Python using the latest stateless specification and Python SDK v2. With the 2026-07-28 MCP specification revision, the core protocol became stateless, making it easier to scale on standard HTTP infrastructure without persistent session connections. As the official Python SDK transitioned to v2, it provides a high-level MCPServer API that makes it simple to define tools, resources, and prompts using standard functions and decorators. In Python 3.10 or newer environments, you can define interfaces based on type hints without writing a separate manifest, and debug servers using the local MCP Inspector. 10 Free AI Tools That Replace Expensive Software for Data Scientists Introduces 10 free, open-source AI tools that can replace expensive commercial software for data scientists. Open-source alternatives that match or exceed the performance of expensive enterprise tools such as DataRobot, Tableau, and commercial LLM APIs have advanced to bridge the cost gap. By utilizing Ollama and Open WebUI, users can securely run the latest open-weight language models locally without per-token API costs and collaborate via a web interface. Tabby, a self-hosted AI coding assistant, ensures data sovereignty by preventing code leaks to external servers and serves as an alternative to tools like GitHub Copilot. An Explainable Header-Centric Framework for Large-Scale Semantic Table Interpretation and Data Quality Assessment This study presents an explainable header-centric framework that evaluates semantic table interpretation and data quality for knowledge graph construction using only column header metadata without cell values. Maps column headers to 39 interpretable FinalFormat types through curated vocabulary resources while maintaining token-level traceability. Applies assigned type-specific validation rules to detect missing values, duplicates, domain violations, and synthesizes them into HeadersIQ, a lightweight data quality metric. Demonstrated broad practical coverage over real-world noisy metadata through evaluations on heterogeneous benchmarks, including UCI, Kaggle, and SemTab 2024, comprising around 120,000 header columns. Synthesis Through Simulation: Generating Coherent Enterprise Data via Scalable Agent-System Interaction This research introduces Synthesis Through Simulation (STS), a new paradigm for synthesizing data without schemas while ensuring the structural validity of enterprise data through API interactions within simulated environments. To overcome the constraints of enterprise systems and data schemas, the authors propose STS, a schema-free data synthesis paradigm that generates data by interacting with policy-enforced APIs. Generalist Populator (GP), a domain-agnostic agent of STS, achieved an average marginal fidelity of 0.88 and 100% constraint satisfaction across 10 environments without direct database schema access. It demonstrated superior performance even in environments where traditional statistical synthesizers failed due to seed data constraints or where schema-based agents exhibited high trajectory failure rates (82%), and the entire framework and dataset have been open-sourced. Agent-Controlled Forgetting for Tool-Using Agents: Reversible Context Curation in Practice This paper investigates an agent-controlled forgetting technique where tool-using AI agents replace previous observations with summary notes and preserve the original content in a recoverable archive. Developed a Python harness that allows tool-using agents to replace previous observations with concise notes and recover them when necessary, without requiring model training. Significantly lowered API costs in OpenTelemetry debugging and implementation task tests by reducing provider-reported prompt tokens from 912,492 to 231,951 and cutting cumulative input tokens by 50%. Observed an increase in request counts and a 17% time delay, confirming that workload dependency is crucial as some task types yielded no cost savings or suffered degraded quality. The Harness as the Only Mutable Surface: Compliance-Bounded Self-Evolution of LLM Agents in Credit Pipelines, with a Measured Admission Gate This study introduces a measured admission gate and bounds the self-evolution of LLM agents by freezing model weights and modifying only runtime harnesses, enabling regulatory compliance review in credit pipelines. To enable regulatory oversight and auditing, the agent's scope of self-evolution is restricted solely to the runtime harness (prompt text, tool-calling logic, etc.) rather than model weights, structuring all changes as traceable diffs. A dual-loop engine was designed based on an admission gate that records pre-deployment hash chains, successfully blocking the introduction of harmful changes. The mechanism is mapped to high-risk credit assessment regulations under the EU AI Act, while pointing out that the April 2026 US model-risk guidance excludes agentic AI from its scope. Plan-and-Patch: Diffusion Language Models for Agentic Planning This study proposes the Plan-and-Patch framework, which leverages diffusion language models (dLLMs) for long-horizon agent tasks to generate initial plans in parallel and locally repair only the parts requiring revision while preserving the surrounding context. Introduced an infilling approach that modifies only specific segments while preserving prefix and suffix structures, instead of regenerating the entire plan from scratch. In zero-shot evaluations on the Natural Plan benchmark, the diffusion model achieved a plan repair success rate of 53.7%, approximately twice as high as the autoregressive (AR) model's 27.0%. In post-training evaluations on ALFWorld and TextCraft benchmarks, it maintained generation success rates comparable to autoregressive models while reducing plan generation latency by 39–46%. Freeze the Decoder, Heal the Encoder: Parameter-Efficient Adaptation for SVD-Based KV-Cache Compression This study investigates the efficiency of training only the encoder when fine-tuning SVD-based KV-cache compression models, and identifies the evaluation distortion issue caused by applying identical learning rates when comparing fine-tuning methods with differing parameter counts. It demonstrated that comparing fine-tuning methods with substantially different numbers of trainable parameters using the same learning rate leads to an evaluation artifact where methods with fewer parameters artificially appear superior. When tuning optimal learning rates for each setting, freezing the decoder and training (healing) only the encoder maintains performance on par with full fine-tuning while reducing trainable parameters and optimizer memory by 3x. These memory savings and performance parity were verified across multiple seed iterations on a vision-language model (Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct) and two text-only backbone models. SPERA: Spherical Prior EEG Foundation Model with Geometry- and Frequency-Aware Latent Prediction SPERA, a general-purpose electroencephalography (EEG) foundation model incorporating spherical spatial priors and frequency-aware latent prediction, has been proposed to accommodate diverse electrode placements and subject environments. Introduced a Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) to perform predictions in latent space, overcoming the limitations of conventional signal reconstruction-based methods Combined Legendre polynomial spatial priors to encode varied scalp electrode geometries, spatio-temporal decomposed attention, and a spectral view alignment regularizer Achieved state-of-the-art average balanced accuracy across nine downstream tasks spanning clinical, cognitive, and brain-computer interface (BCI) domains, while demonstrating exceptional parameter efficiency during linear probing Coverage, Not Difficulty, Sets How Much Synthetic Data an Activation Probe Needs A study showing that the amount of synthetic data required to train activation probes for monitoring deployed language models is determined by cross-concept coverage (transferability) rather than the difficulty of individual types. Probes for monitoring high-risk situations and toxicity reach peak performance with only about 80 synthetic samples on Gemma-3-27B-IT, whereas instruction-following failure monitoring probes require several times more samples. The median number of own-kind samples needed to reach half-gain saturation across evaluation distributions is similar at 7-11 samples for all three concepts, indicating that differences in individual difficulty are minimal. The effect of data diversity (breadth) depends on concept transferability; while one high-risk concept can largely cover others, instruction-following failure concepts are difficult to substitute for one another, making a diverse range of data types essential. When Routing Reveals Membership: Privacy Leakage from MoE Router Telemetry This study demonstrates that router telemetry data used for monitoring and debugging MoE language models can leak whether specific data was used during fine-tuning. Proposed a novel membership inference attack (MIA) method that combines routing telemetry collected from Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models with conventional output signals. Confirmed persistent privacy leakage across three MoE architectures and three data domains, showing an improvement of 2.7 to 9.4 percentage points in TPR at 1% FPR when utilizing router telemetry. Observed consistent information leakage even under various training setups—such as frozen-router settings, LoRA, and instruction tuning—as well as under constrained telemetry environments. Phase-HDC: Replacing Optimizer History with Gradient Thresholds in Discrete Phase Learning This paper proposes Phase-HDC, a technique that trains hyperdimensional classifiers without storing additional memory beyond the model by utilizing gradient thresholds instead of the optimizer's past gradient history. Phase-HDC operates on a simple rule of rotating the stored angle by at most one step in the direction opposite to the sign of the gradient, only when the current gradient is sufficiently large. Across 11 image, tabular, and text datasets, it achieved 16–23x less state storage compared to standard float32 Adam and 4–6x less compared to 8-bit Adam. Although there is an accuracy loss of about 5 percentage points compared to float32 Adam, it recorded higher accuracy than 8-bit Adam across 6 datasets, including byte-level text prediction where 8-bit Adam collapses. Diffu-LoRA: A Novel Low-Rank Adaptation for Personalized Diffusion Models Proposes the Diffu-LoRA framework, which utilizes a gate-based Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) technique to optimally and dynamically allocate layer-wise adaptation capacity for personalized text-to-image diffusion models. Presents Diffu-LoRA, which integrates learnable gates to solve the challenge of allocating layer-wise adaptation capacity when personalizing diffusion models with a few reference images. Separately learns adaptation weights and gate parameters via bilevel optimization, and eliminates unnecessary elements through progressive pruning to fit a designated rank budget. Simultaneously enhances subject identity preservation and prompt fidelity compared to conventional fine-tuning baselines on Stable Diffusion and DreamBooth datasets while keeping the pre-trained backbone frozen. Wieszcz-XIX: A 3.1-Billion-Word Corpus of Pre-1918 Polish and Temporally Bounded Language Models Trained From Scratch This research focuses on the construction of 'Wieszcz-XIX', a 3.1-billion-word corpus consisting of Polish literature published between 1800 and 1918, and temporally bounded language models trained from scratch on it. Constructed and applied a cleaning pipeline to 'Wieszcz-XIX', a historical Polish corpus from 1800 to 1918 comprising 6.75 billion tokens (approximately 3.1 billion words, 294,369 documents), which is more than 1,000 times larger than existing machine-readable annotated corpora. Trained decoder-only language models ranging from 47 million (47M) to 349 million (349M) parameters from scratch using this corpus, demonstrating their ability to preserve period vocabulary and orthography. Showed a higher bit-cost for post-1918 vocabulary compared to modern Polish models while faithfully reproducing contemporary vocabulary and spelling, and publicly released the entire corpus, code, and model weights. Large Language Model-Assisted Preparation of Transportation Management Plans: A Case Study with WisDOT WisTMP System A study on an open-source Large Language Model (LLM) fine-tuning framework to automate the preparation of Transportation Management Plans (TMP) for work zones, based on the Wisconsin Department of Transportation (WisDOT) WisTMP system. Fine-tuned local open-source LLMs for data security by converting historical WisTMP PDF documents into structured JSON question-answering datasets. While fine-tuning significantly improved standard text generation metrics, it showed limitations in over-generating strategies, providing project-specific justifications, and producing accurate cost estimations. Scaling the model size from 7B/8B to 14B showed limited performance improvements. Cognitive Thermometers: Machine Learning and Logical Complexity This paper proposes an integrated complexity analysis framework that measures human semantic category representations and the semantic preference principles of natural language using machine learning as 'cognitive thermometers' instead of symbolic logic. Existing explanations based on logical definability and complexity have limitations in that they are overly sensitive to the choice of logical language. Machine learning and logic often show convergent results regarding relative complexity and semantic typological effects; when they conflict, machine learning models provide better explanations. By regarding machine learning models as 'cognitive thermometers,' an integrated complexity approach connecting symbolic logic and connectionist AI can be established. Lossy Compressive Text Autoencoders This study proposes a lossy compressive text autoencoder architecture that combines data compression and representation learning to efficiently compress and reconstruct web text data. Proposes an autoencoder architecture featuring residual downscaling and upscaling of hidden representations along the temporal axis, along with a residual low-dimensional discrete bottleneck structure Analyzes the similarity between original and reconstructed texts across various compression levels at both surface level (BLEU) and semantic level (LLM-based evaluation) Validates the model's reconstruction quality and task performance through evaluations on downstream question answering and semantic textual similarity benchmarks Let AI Agents Draw Big Arrows, Boxes, and Text on Your Screen Introducing big-arrow-on-the-screen, a macOS CLI tool that allows AI agents to visually guide users with large arrows and text on the screen toward UI elements requiring manual human interaction, such as permission approvals or payments. Visually guides users by pointing to on-screen UI elements with arrows or boxes during steps requiring human judgment and manual action, such as permission grants, 2FA, and payment confirmations. An open-source (MIT) tool built as a single Swift binary that passes overlay clicks through to the underlying application rather than performing direct clicks or inputs. Supports agent skills for Claude Code and Codex, and provides target tracking based on coordinates, windows, or UI element names, along with voice guidance (--say). Show GN: Hacker News(paper) - Summarizes Hacker News every morning in a newspaper format Introduction and open-source release of Hacker News(paper), a web service that translates and summarizes the top 30 Hacker News posts from the previous day into Korean every morning in a paper newspaper style. Collects posts and comments up to the top 30 from the previous day via Hacker News past tab, providing Korean translations and summaries Arranges top posts with images and summaries for quick viewing, inspired by domestic newspaper layouts Continuously refining prompts to enhance translation and summary quality, with source code released on GitHub OpenAI, the Partition Principle, and Mathematics Set theory expert Asaf Karagila strongly criticized OpenAI's preprint on mathematical research related to the partition principle, citing poor communication quality and failure to meet academic standards. Although OpenAI published results stating that the partition principle does not imply the axiom of choice, expert Asaf Karagila pointed out that it is at a level that warrants desk rejection due to unclear exposition, strange structure, and inappropriate references. He criticized the practice of releasing massive amounts of incomprehensible AI-generated solutions and expecting peer review, likening it to a 'Denial of Service (DoS) attack' that paralyzes researchers' primary work. He emphasized the need for the mathematical community to establish evaluation criteria for contributions and for AI companies to adhere to academic communication standards, so that AI research achievements are not exaggerated into misleading claims about replacing mathematicians. What You Need to Know About Jev, Decision-Making AI for GTM TypeSafe AI, founded by a co-inventor of ChatGPT, has unveiled Jev, a decision-specialized AI model that focuses on fast classification and judgment based on set criteria rather than text generation. Instead of generating text, Jev automates repetitive decision-making tasks such as GTM by returning classifications, scoring, and yes/no probabilities based on defined criteria. Up to 193x faster and 445x cheaper than frontier LLMs, it outputs calibrated confidence and structured responses in a single shot via RLCD training. Rather than fully replacing LLMs, it acts as a complement that selectively executes judgments across research tasks or large-scale tabular data, allowing LLMs to generate content only for items that pass. Claude Startups Program to Review All Applications Anthropic announced that it will review all applications due to exceeding the limit for free benefits in the Claude Startups program. The allocation limits for a 1-year free subscription to Claude Team and $1,000 in API credits have currently been exceeded. Existing members can continue to enjoy benefits such as the Startup Stack, Applied AI office hours, and events. This notice has been added to the FAQ section on the official Claude Startups page. Clarification ARR Commitment [D] A researcher's inquiry regarding whether a revised PDF incorporating reviewers' feedback needs to be submitted in advance when committing an ARR-reviewed paper to a conference. The author intends to submit an ARR-reviewed paper to EACL 2027 and is inquiring about the PDF attachment requirements. They are confused about whether minor reviewer feedback should be incorporated into the PDF for this submission in advance, or if they should wait until the camera-ready stage after final acceptance. They previously understood that providing only the review link would suffice, but are now inquiring about additional procedures. I built MaRN: a PyTorch library for training neural networks through low-dimensional parameter mappings [P] MaRN, a PyTorch library that enables training neural networks via low-dimensional latent representations instead of directly learning all model parameters, has been released. MaRN provides a mapping method that optimizes compact latent representations rather than directly training model parameters. In the MNIST CNN benchmark, it significantly reduced the parameter count by up to 131.8 times while minimizing the drop in accuracy to around 1 percentage point. Although training speed can be considerably slower and performance variations exist across tasks, it supports global and layer-wise mappings, regularization, and pruning features. I built ALHR: A tree based sparse attention system that achieves sub-quadratic inference while retaining accuracy. [P] This article discusses the development of ALHR, a sparse attention system that achieves sub-quadratic (NlogN) inference complexity while preserving accuracy by leveraging static binary trees and learnable routing functions. ALHR (Adaptive Learnable Hierarchical Routing) drastically reduces the number of keys required to be read per query using a static binary tree structure and learning functions. In the 1024-token MQAR test, it maintained 92.1% Top-1 accuracy compared to the Dense method, demonstrating 35.3x KV compression and linear VRAM scaling. Limitations include that the training process still has quadratic complexity and full-scale testing has not yet been completed. [2610.08927] Can AI Agents Make Open-Ended Scientific Discovery? Evidence from Station This study verifies that AI agents can autonomously perform scientific discovery in an open-ended environment called Station through a supervisor mechanism and meta-reflection. A Supervisor mechanism and periodic Meta Reflection were proposed to enhance exploration persistence in open-ended scientific discovery environments lacking intermediate evaluation metrics. In an evaluation using research questions from three ICLR oral presentation papers, Station substantially outperformed comparison systems by rediscovering an average of 62.7% of existing research result criteria. Even in open-ended tasks without reference papers, some discoveries derived by the agent closely matched findings published by real-world researchers after the knowledge cutoff date. ThinkingBox: Solving an agent task once vs. solving it 20/20: 507 stateful workflows graded on terminal database state [R] Introduction to the ThinkingBox research benchmark, which evaluates AI agents based on the final database state to examine the gap between single-run success rates and repeated execution reliability. Evaluated final backend states and side effects across 507 workflows in 5 business domains through a total of 10,140 attempts (20 runs per model). Model rankings were observed to almost completely invert when evaluated on solving at least once (pass@20) versus succeeding 20 consecutive times (all-20). 67.24% of failed attempts appeared to terminate normally, but actual state inspections revealed issues such as incorrect field values and unintended side effects. Rocket Report: A rare Falcon 9 scrub; is it just about Vulcan's time to shine? A report summarizing recent key developments in the global space rocket industry, including United Launch Alliance's preparations for the Vulcan rocket's return to flight and the successful fifth launch of South Korea's Nuri rocket. United Launch Alliance (ULA) has scheduled the return-to-flight date for its Vulcan rocket for the Amazon Leo mission no earlier than October 29, with an additional launch planned within the year. South Korea successfully deployed key satellites, including five national security ultra-small satellites, via the fifth launch of its independently developed Nuri rocket. Among the 10 CubeSat secondary payloads aboard the Nuri rocket, 9 were normally deployed, but startup Quaternion's PERSAT-02 failed to separate. Presentation: Ontology‐Driven Observability: Building the E2E Knowledge Graph at Netflix Scale Presents a case study of Netflix building an end-to-end observability framework using ontology-based knowledge graphs and AI agent workflows in a large-scale environment processing 38 million events per second. Moved away from reactive monitoring systems to introduce ontology and AI agent workflows powered by Claude and graph databases. Integrated MELT (Metrics, Events, Logs, Traces) telemetry data into a queryable knowledge graph to implement automated triage, root cause analysis, and self-healing systems. Addresses data engineering and observability challenges in massive traffic environments, such as handling a peak traffic of 65 million concurrent streams during the Jake Paul vs. Mike Tyson boxing match in late 2024. Anthropic Launches Free AI Vulnerability Scanner for Open-Source Projects Anthropic has launched 'OSS Scanner', a free vulnerability scanner powered by high-performance AI models to secure the open-source software ecosystem. Leverages top-tier AI models such as Claude Mythos to provide periodic, thorough, and free security scans for open-source projects. Project maintainers can apply to participate by submitting a pull request to the GitHub repository with a YAML file containing Dockerfile paths and configurations. Considering the potential for false positives in AI-based detection, it does not enforce an immediate 90-day disclosure deadline, unlike traditional vulnerability disclosure programs. Flax Typhoon Exploits Five Flaws as CISA Sets October 11 Deadline for Federal Agencies The U.S. CISA added five security vulnerabilities exploited by China-linked hacking group Flax Typhoon to its KEV catalog and ordered federal agencies to take action by October 11. CISA added five vulnerabilities related to ProFTPD, ONLYOFFICE Docs, Strapi, Apache Struts, and ISC BIND (including CVE-2015-3306) exploited by Flax Typhoon to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog. According to a multinational joint cybersecurity advisory from the United States, Australia, the United Kingdom, and others, an attack campaign backed by Chinese company Integrity Technology Group is exploiting a total of eight vulnerabilities for initial access and data exfiltration. U.S. federal agencies must apply the relevant security patches or discontinue use of the affected products by October 11, 2026, in response to active exploitation threats. The AI Velocity Paradox: Why Security Is Decades Behind AI Ambition Covers report findings showing that while enterprises are rapidly adopting autonomous AI agents, they remain stuck in legacy human-centric security architectures, creating a critical security gap. According to SailPoint's 'Horizons of Identity Security' report, 60% of all enterprises remain in early stages of security maturity (Horizon 1 or Horizon 2), hitting architectural limitations. While human identity security improved with Horizon 1 representation dropping from 45% five years ago to 23% today, security for AI agents and non-human identities significantly lags behind, with 54% still remaining in Horizon 1. Applying periodic access review processes designed for humans to machine identities that spin up and down in seconds or minutes is inefficient, demanding a paradigm shift toward continuous, automated policy enforcement operating at machine speed. Three Teams Demonstrate Remote Hacks of Fully Patched Google Pixel 10 at Pwn2Own At the Pwn2Own Ireland hacking competition, three research teams successfully demonstrated remote hacks against the Google Pixel 10 smartphone running the latest security patches. At the Pwn2Own Ireland competition, three out of four remote hacking attempts targeting the fully patched Google Pixel 10 succeeded, resulting in a total payout of $562,500 in prize money. Ikotas Labs claimed the overall championship, earning the competition's highest single prize of $300,000 and 30 master-of-pwn points for their Pixel 10 hack demonstration. Details of the demonstrated vulnerabilities were disclosed to the manufacturer, Google, under competition rules, with technical details scheduled for public release after a 90-day patching period. FBI Seizes 7 Domains, Disrupts Flax Typhoon Tools Used in Critical Infrastructure Intrusions The U.S. FBI and the Department of Justice seized and disrupted seven domains and malicious tools used by Flax Typhoon, a China-linked hacking group involved in intrusions against critical infrastructure. The FBI and the U.S. Department of Justice neutralized key C2 domains and reconnaissance platforms of Flax Typhoon, which is linked to the Chinese government contractor Integrity Technology Group. The group attacked global networks utilizing Mirai variant-based IoT botnets, the Sparrow management application, MicroScan containing over 1,300 penetration testing scripts, and the spear-phishing tool FishHub. Victims included power companies in the United States, airports in Japan and Poland, natural gas and power infrastructure companies in Taiwan, and around 20 universities. Anthropic's 'Claude Science' Completes Humanity's First Ultraviolet All-Sky Map Using Anthropic's research AI agent 'Claude Science', humanity's first ultraviolet all-sky map has been completed, reconstructing cosmic ultraviolet regions that were previously unobserved gaps. Anthropic researchers and Professor Brice Menard of Johns Hopkins University deployed 'Claude Science' multi-agents to cross-calibrate and process legacy satellite observation data in parallel. The one-third of cosmic regions with missing data was reconstructed with high precision within an error margin of approximately 10% using machine learning inpainting techniques and statistical regression models. The project demonstrated the potential of collaboration between expert oversight and AI autonomy, as researchers reviewed instrument-specific atmospheric airglow errors missed by the AI agent and immediately corrected 38,000 data points. Anthropic Unveils 'Claude Dashboard and Motion'…\"Enterprise Data Analysis and Video Production\" Anthropic has launched betas for 'Claude Dashboard', which generates real-time data dashboards using natural language, and 'Claude Motion', which produces code-based animated explanatory videos. Beta launch of 'Claude Dashboard', which connects with major data platforms (BigQuery, Snowflake, etc.) via natural language queries to generate real-time dashboards Unveiling of 'Claude Motion', which visualizes data into explanatory videos (MP4) without hallucinations through code-based animations using React, HTML5, CSS, and more Official release and full expansion to include existing document, presentation, and design tools (Claude Docs, Slides, Design) in the free plan \"AI Catches Open Source Vulnerabilities\"... Anthropic Unveils Free Security Scanning Tool Anthropic has unveiled 'OSS Scanner', a free AI inspection tool that detects security vulnerabilities in open source software and supports remediation patches. Launch of 'OSS Scanner', a free tool that leverages Anthropic's latest high-performance models to regularly inspect participating open source projects and provide root causes of vulnerabilities along with remediation patches Pilot operation results showed that 88% (85 cases) out of 97 high-risk and critical vulnerabilities across 48 projects met verification criteria, confirming positive effects in projects such as PostgreSQL and wolfSSL Aiming to strengthen the security of critical national infrastructure and the open source ecosystem, supporting participation through PR review focused on projects with significant impact on infrastructure and user security Three Fired OpenAI Researchers Urge: \"AI Reasoning Oversight Capabilities Must Be Preserved\" Three safety researchers dismissed from OpenAI sent an open letter urging the company to strengthen collaboration with external safety evaluation organizations and maintain monitoring capabilities over AI reasoning processes. The three dismissed safety researchers criticized OpenAI's sudden dismissals, stating that the move creates a chilling effect among internal employees and hinders safety verification collaboration. They warned that the latest 'Loop Transformer' architecture makes monitoring Chain of Thought (CoT) difficult, which could neutralize danger detection safety systems. The researchers demanded that OpenAI allow independent evaluations by external safety evaluation bodies, preserve overseability, and maintain a transparent culture of communication; however, the company stated that policy violations regarding the handling of sensitive information were the reason for the dismissals. OpenAI Lowers Annual Revenue Estimate by $20B... Big Tech Stocks Tumble in Tandem Global tech stocks plummeted as OpenAI's actual annual recurring revenue (ARR) was revealed to be approximately $20 billion lower than previous market estimates due to differences in calculation methods. OpenAI disclosed that its ARR as of late September stood at approximately $50 billion, falling short of previous market expectations of $68 billion to $70 billion due to accounting discrepancies, such as the inclusion of cloud partner revenues. The revenue revision reignited concerns over an AI bubble and profitability, triggering broad declines in the Nasdaq 100 index and shares of major AI infrastructure and semiconductor companies, including Nvidia and Oracle. While OpenAI continues to post robust growth, including a 77% increase in Q3 revenue, and is in discussions to secure $30 billion in new funding, efforts toward an IPO appear to be delayed amid ongoing safety controversies. Meta Deploys LLMs to Detect Ads Directing to Child Sexual Abuse Material Meta has introduced an LLM-based detection system and red-teaming AI agents to block tactics that use benign-looking ads to direct users to external illegal sites, and announced major enforcement results against child sexual abuse material. Meta took action on 33.2 million pieces of child sexual abuse content on Facebook and Instagram in the first half of this year, proactively detecting over 97% with its own systems. To detect 'signposting' techniques that disguise links as benign ads to direct users to external child sexual abuse sites, Meta introduced an LLM system that inspects link destinations. Meta strengthened platform safety measures, including operating 'red-team AI agents' to identify system weaknesses and blocking attempts to circumvent account penalties. [AI Highway] Corporate AI Enters the Private Era: 'Modular Data Centers' on the Rise As enterprises adopt their own private AI environments to strengthen data control and security, modular AI data centers that enable rapid deployment and expansion are emerging as an alternative. To enhance security and reduce costs, demand for private AI is increasing—lowering reliance on external clouds while concurrently operating in-house AI environments and commercial models. Driven by performance improvements in open models and fine-tuning efficiency, the competitiveness of enterprise-tailored infrastructure operations is rising. Modular AI data centers, capable of rapidly accommodating high-density GPU environments and allowing phased scaling, are gaining attention as independent infrastructure solutions. Smart City Expo Emerges as a Key Global Urban Summit for 2026, Attended by Mayors Worldwide from Paris to Freetown Mayors from across the globe and leading global big tech companies will gather in large numbers at the Smart City Expo World Congress (SCEWC) 2026 held in Barcelona to discuss urban innovation, housing, and climate solutions. Smart City Expo World Congress 2026 will be held in Barcelona, Spain, from November 3 to 5 under the theme 'Urban Solutions, Real Impact'. A large delegation of mayors from four continents—including former Mayor of Paris Anne Hidalgo, alongside representatives from Atlanta, Athens, and Milan—as well as leading futurists and experts such as Nikolas Badminton, will be in attendance. More than 1,100 global companies and organizations, including Google, Microsoft, NVIDIA, and Dell, will participate, bringing together approximately 27,000 attendees. Shockwaves Hit Semiconductor Stocks Amid Projections of \"OpenAI Revenue Below Expectations\" Major U.S. stock indices and semiconductor-related tech stocks dropped across the board following news that OpenAI's projected annualized revenue fell short of market expectations. The annualized projected revenue OpenAI shared with investors was $50 billion, falling roughly $20 billion short of the market forecast of $70 billion. This fueled growing investor anxiety over actual demand relative to AI infrastructure investment costs, causing the Nasdaq composite to fall 1.25%. Shares of major semiconductor and tech companies also tumbled simultaneously, including Nvidia (-2.9%), Intel (-5.3%), Oracle (-5.5%), and AMD (-3.9%)."